There are two inputs which demonstrate an explicit surface being
converted to an implicit surface. Optionally, an image of each surface
can also be rendered by uncommenting the "dump image" and "dump
modify" commands in the inputs. In the images, the outline of the
surface is in grey. The particles are also drawn in the 2D case but
not in the 3D case.

1) The in.exp2imp.circle.2d input is a 2D circle which is converted to
   an implicit surface.
2) The in.exp2imp.sphere.3d input is a 3D sphere which is converted to
   an implicit surface

There are also several 2D axisymmetric inputs built around a 10-degree
sphere-cone body of revolution (data.spherecone, generated by
gen_spherecone.py):

3) in.exp2imp.axi.circle converts an explicit circle to implicit
   surfaces in an axisymmetric domain.
4) in.exp2imp.axi.spherecone converts the explicit sphere-cone to
   implicit surfaces.
5) in.exp2imp.axi.spherecone.readback repeats the conversion and writes
   the corner point values to tmp.sc.corners.dat with write_isurf.
6) in.exp2imp.axi.spherecone.readback2 reads that file back with
   read_isurf and reproduces the identical body; run the readback input
   first to create it.
7) in.ablate.axi.spherecone recedes the sphere-cone with flux-driven
   ablation; it writes the initial and final surfaces to
   tmp.sc.ablate.start.surf and tmp.sc.ablate.end.surf for plotting.
8) in.deposit.axi.spherecone grows the sphere-cone instead, with
   fix ablate in mode deposit driven by the incident mass flux.
